es, Shenyang, People's Republic of China\nL99: \nL100: 【8†Search for more papers by this author】\nL101: \nL102: 【9†Zaili Dong】,\nL103: \nL104: Zaili Dong\nL105: \nL106: State Key Laboratory of Robotics, Shenyang Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Shenyang, People's Republic of China\nL107: 【9†Search for more papers by this author】\nL108: \nL109: 【10†Lianqing Liu】,\nL110: \nL111: Lianqing Liu\nL112: \nL113: State Key Laboratory of Robotics, Shenyang Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Shenyang, People's Republic of China\nL114: \nL115: 【10†Search for more papers by this author】\nL116: \nL117: 【11†Osamu Tabata】,\nL118: \nL119: Corresponding Author\nL120: \nL121: Osamu Tabata\nL122: \nL123:   * 【15†[email protected]】\nL124: \nL125: Department of Micro Engineering, Kyoto University, Kyoto, Japan\nL126: \nL127: Freiburg Institute for Advanced Studies (FRIAS), University of Freiburg, Freiburg, Germany\nL128: 【11†Search for more papers by this author】\nL129: \nL130: 【13†Wen J. Li】,\nL131: \nL132: Corresponding Author\nL133: \nL134: Wen J. Li\nL135: \nL136:   * 【16†[email protected]】\nL137: \nL138: State Key Laboratory of Robotics, Shenyang Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Shenyang, People's Republic of China\nL139: \nL140: Deparment of Mechanical and Biomedical Engineering, City University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, People's Republic of China\nL141: \nL142: 【13†Search for more papers by this author】\nL143: \nL144: First published: 01 October 2013\nL145: 【17†https://doi.org/10.1049/mnl.2013.0483Digital Object Identifier (DOI)†doi.org】\nL146: \nL147: view metrics\nL148: \nL149: Sections\nL150: \nL151: CITE\nL152: \nL153: 【18†Tools】\nL154: \nL155: Share full-text access\nL156: \nL157: Please review our 【19†Terms and Conditions of Use†onlinelibrary.wiley.com】 and check box below to share full-text version of article.\nL158: \nL159: [Input]I have read and accept the Wiley Online Library Terms and Conditions of Use\nL160: \nL161: * * *\nL162: \nL163: Shareable Link\nL164: Use the link below to share a full-text version of this article with your friends and colleagues. 【20†Learn more.†onlinelibrary.wiley.com】\nL165: \nL166: [Button: Copy URL]\nL167: \nL168: Share a link\nL169: \nL170: Share on\nL171: ## Abstract\nL172: The nanoscale manipulation of 1D soft and flexible ‘DNA origami nanotubes’ (DONs) that are 6 nm in diameter and 400 nm in length, and placed on a mica substrate in a TE/Mg^{2+} buffer solution, was executed by an atomic force microscopy (AFM) tip using a programmable tapping-mode manipulation strategy. The AFM's tip tapping amplitude was controlled to be less than or equal to 10 nm while vibrating in a solution with Mg^{2+} concentration of 10–20 mM.\nL173: A series of single-point and one-step manipulation experiments revealed that manipulation can be achieved with an 80% successful rate under the condition that the AFM tip vibration amplitude is 3–4 nm and the Mg^{2+} concentration is 10 mM. Utilising this optimised condition, multipoint and multistep manipulation based on the programmable tapping-mode AFM process was conducted and nanomanipulation of DONs was successfully demonstrated.\nL174: ## 1 Introduction\nL175: Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) origami [【21†[1]】] has attracted attention as an excellent platform to arrange various nanomaterials, such as metallic nanoparticles, carbon nanotubes (CNTs) and so on [【22†[2]】-【23†[4]】], precisely using the base sequence information of a scaffold DNA. Moreover, DNA origami can bind with each other with the help of hybridisation of DNA strands arranged at its edges and form higher-order structures of micrometre-scale dimensions [【24†[5]】].\nL176: Based on these features of DNA origami, we proposed to use DNA origami as nanoscale functional building blocks and place them on microelectromechanical systems (MEMS) to realise nano/microengineered and molecular systems (NEMS) [【25†[6]】]. The concept of this proposed technique is termed as programmable oriented self-assembly on MEMS (P-OSAM) [【26†[7]】] and is illustrated in Fig. 【27†1】.\nL177: As shown, a micrometre-scale functional structure is integrated on a MEMS substrate by the oriented self-assembly of multiple nanoscale functional building blocks made of DNA origami functionalised by nanomaterials. To accomplish this concept, the placement of the initial DNA origami as an origin of the subsequent oriented self-assembly at the desired position is a critical process.\nL178: In this Letter, we propose a new approach utilising the programmable tapping-mode atomic force microscopy (AFM) manipulation technique developed by our group [【28†[8]】, 【29†[9]】] to place the initial DNA origami in a desired position in a fluidic medium.\nL179: Figure 1\nL180: \nL181: 【18†Open in figure viewer】【30†PowerPoint】\nL182: \nL183: Illustration of the proposed programmable oriented self-assembly on MEMS (P-OSAM) concept\nL184: \nL185: a Initial DNA origami is manipulated to a desired position by using a programmable tapping-mode AFM tip on MEMS substrates\nL186: \nL187: b Assembly of AuNPs decorated DNA origami is performed by oriented self-assembly\nL188: \nL189: c Plasmon waveguides below the diffraction limit of light can be constructed\nL190: Atomic force microscopy (AFM) [【31†[10]】] has become the most popular tool for researchers in the research areas of nanoimaging and nanomanipulation. It can be applied not only for imaging and studying the surface topography in nanoscale, but also for the manipulation, assembling and cutting of nanoscale objects. After the first demonstration by Schaefer et al. [【32†[11]】] and Junno et al. [【33†[12]】] in 1995, AFM-based nanomanipulation has been widely used by many groups.\nL191: Many kinds of nanoscale objects were manipulated or assembled into special patterns in recent years. For example, Xi's group [【34†[13]】] pushed latex particles and silver nanorods on a glass surface using their augmented reality-based AFM by contact-mode, which could display real-time changes in the environment during manipulation using a force model between the tip and sample's surface. Prior to Xi's work, Ritter et al.\nL192: [【35†[14]】] also demonstrated the pushing of antimony particles on a graphite surface using contact-mode manipulation. However, manipulation approaches based on contact-mode have the drawback of wearing out the AFM tip easily as the contact force is significant, which makes continuous nanomanipulation difficult.\nL193: Furthermore, it is difficult to obtain clear nanoscale images after contact-mode manipulation, especially for DNA molecules in liquid which needs sharp AFM tips in order to obtain good image quality. Hence, tapping-mode manipulation was proposed to solve this problem and was found to have a higher success rate of nanoscale manipulation and imaging. Resch et al.\nL194: [【36†[15]】] utilised the tapping-mode AMF tip to push a nanoparticle by decreasing the vibration set point, while the Z-feedback was turned off during the manipulation process. Liu et al. [【37†[16]】] developed a new tapping-mode-based AFM manipulation method, which can push nanoparticles by changing the amplitude set point, scanned area, aspect ratio of the scanned area and the scanning angle. However, their method has been only applied for particles made of hard (i.e. low elasticity) materials.\nL195: To the best of our knowledge, no one has attempted to manipulate soft and flexible material with a length of a few hundred nanometres such as DNA origami nanotubes (DONs) in liquid. To address this task, multipoint and multistep manipulation is required, which is implemented by the programmable tapping-mode AFM manipulation method [【28†[8]】, 【29†[9]】] with optimised manipulation parameters for DONs.\nL196: As a first step to verify the feasibility of using the programmable tapping-mode AFM manipulation method (i.e. where the AFM tip's trajectory, vibration amplitude and moving speed are controlled by an algorithm) to manipulate DONs in a fluidic medium, comprehensive experiments were conducted and the results are presented in this Letter.\nL197: The effects of two important manipulating parameters – manipulation amplitude of the AFM tip and Mg^{2+} concentration of the fluidic medium – on the nanomanipulation results are presented, including the optimised values of these parameters as determined experimentally. Section 2 describes the sample preparation and the AFM nanomanipulating method. Section 3 summarises and discusses the experimental results.\nL198: ## 2 Material and experimental method\nL199: ### 2.1 DON and AFM\nL200: \nL201: The size of the DONs used in the experiments shown in Fig. 【38†2e】 is 6 nm in diameter and 400 nm in length as reported by Kiss et al. [【39†[17]】]. The TE/Mg^{2+} (20 mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.6, 1 mM EDTA, 10–20 mM MgCl_{2}) buffer solution containing DON were deposited onto freshly cleaved mica. Imaging and manipulation were both conducted by a tapping-mode AFM system (Digital Instrument Nanoscope III) which is a Bruker Multimode AFM with a E scanner head and DNP tip.\nL202: \nL203: Figure 2\nL204: 【18†Open in figure viewer】【40†PowerPoint】\nL205: \nL206: Structure of the DNA origami nanotubes used in our experiments\nL207: \nL208: a DONs used are 6 nm in diameters\nL209: \nL210: b 28 repeating staple pattern units\nL211: \nL212: c M13mp18 scaffold path\nL213: \nL214: d DNA staple pattern\nL215: \nL216: e AFM image\nL217: ### 2.2 Manipulation procedure\nL218: Fig. 【41†3】 illustrates the trajectory of an AFM tip during the programmable tapping-mode AFM manipulation. The programmable tapping-mode manipulation method plans the trajectory, the manipulating amplitude and the velocity of a tip by a program. The AFM tip vibrates during imaging and manipulation with a pre-determined amplitude similar to the typical operation of the AFM tapping-mode scanning method. The manipulation work reported here is conducted according to the following steps.\nL219: (i) Deposit 2 μl DONs-dispersed buffer solution on a mica surface, wait for 5 min and then scan the sample with the AFM tip in the buffer solution. (ii) Choose a target DON to be manipulated from the scanned image. (iii) Set values of tip displacement, distance of manipulation and vibration amplitude and speed. (iv) Execute the manipulation process. (v) Scan the sample again to check the manipulation result.\nL220: Since the AFM tip knocks the sample surface intermittently during the tapping-mode operation, the time of tip-to-DON contact is shorter than if contact-mode operation were used. Therefore damage to the tip and the sample can be reduced, which is crucial for manipulating soft materials in liquid while keeping a sharp AFM tip for high resolution imaging.\nL221: Figure 3\nL222: \nL223: 【18†Open in figure viewer】【42†PowerPoint】\nL224: \nL225: Conceptual illustration of the programmable tapping-mode AFM manipulating method\nL226: ### 2.3 Manipulation parameters\nL227: As mentioned previously, in this Letter, the concentration of Mg^{2+} of a buffer solution and the manipulating amplitude were found as two important parameters to be optimised, which have a strong influence on the successful rate of manipulating DONs in a fluidic environment.\n"
